# Lazy Stoner Donator Report Provenance

- Treat `reports/` as audit and provenance only. Runtime frontend code must not import or fetch report files.
- Use reports to justify product removals, replacements, availability decisions, price enrichment, and featured-tier decisions.
- Keep report conclusions separate from product facts. A report can explain why a change was made; `wishlist-products.json` remains the live source of truth.
- Availability audits should record method, checked time, verdict, evidence text, HTTP status when available, and buy/add-to-cart signals where relevant.
- Price audits should preserve source and status. If Amazon live extraction is unreliable, prefer user-provided wishlist HTML or documented cached evidence over guessing.
- Alternative-product reports should include accepted and rejected candidates when available. Similarity scores are discovery aids, not final proof of suitability.
- Cleanup reports should list removed titles/ASINs and the remaining count. Use them to verify that requested removals did not leave replaced descendants behind.
- Featured-tier reports should explain why a product is large/featured. Price-based tiers and curated editorial tiers should not be mixed without explicit `featureReason`.
- When a recurring lesson emerges from reports, encode it in `.cursor/rules/`; do not keep re-reading old reports as instructions.
- For relaunch work, create a fresh dated report only when new research, availability checks, price checks, or replacement decisions were actually performed.
